-- title:
--   Full Whittaker integral as a factor Λ(σ)≥ 1 times its block
-- statement:
--   Fix a finite set $S$ of height-one primes of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, a homomorphism $\omega$ from the ideles of $\mathbb{Q}$ to $\mathbb{C}^\times$ all of whose values have modulus $1$, and families $\lambda_1,\lambda_2$ of complex numbers indexed by the primes. The Whittaker transform `whittaker3` used throughout is the triple integral of $f(u(x,y,z)g)\,\psi_{\mathbb{Q}}(-(x+y))$ against the normalised restriction of adelic additive Haar measure to the adelic box of $\mathbb{Q}$, with $u(x,y,z)$ the upper unipotent matrix with entries $x,y,z$ and $\psi_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the standard additive character. Call $f:\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}})\to\mathbb{C}$ admissible if it is continuous, invariant under left translation by the image of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q})$, satisfies $f(z\cdot g)=\omega(z)f(g)$ for central idelic scalars $z$, and at every $p\notin S$ is right invariant under the image of the subgroup of matrices in $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ with all entries of both the matrix and its inverse of valuation $\le 1$, and is a coset eigenfunction with eigenvalue $\lambda_1(p)$, resp. $\lambda_2(p)$, for the Hecke generators $\mathrm{diag}(\varpi_p,1,1)$, resp. $\mathrm{diag}(\varpi_p,\varpi_p,1)$, relative to that subgroup (in the sense that for every finite system of coset representatives of the corresponding double coset the sum of translates equals the eigenvalue times the function). The hypothesis $hE$ asserts: for every $p\notin S$ there is $u:\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathbb{C}$ with $u(0,0)=1$, vanishing off the cone $0\le m_2\le m_1$, such that for every admissible $f$, every $g_0$ commuting with the image of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ and every $m\in\mathbb{Z}^2$, the Whittaker transform of $f$ at $g_0\cdot\mathrm{diag}(p^{m_1},p^{m_2},1)$ (the powers taken of the unit of $\mathbb{Q}_p$ given by the residue characteristic, embedded from $\mathrm{GL}_2$ into $\mathrm{GL}_3$) equals its value at $g_0$ times $u(m)$. The conclusion: there exists $\Lambda:\mathbb{R}\to[0,\infty]$ with $\Lambda(\sigma)\ge 1$ for all $\sigma$, independent of all data below, such that for every admissible $f$, every $n$, coefficients $c:\mathrm{Fin}\,n\to\mathbb{C}$ and translating elements $t:\mathrm{Fin}\,n\to\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}})$ whose archimedean component and whose component at each $p\notin S$ are trivial, and every $\Phi:\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^3\to\mathbb{C}$ which is a product $\Phi(x)=\prod_i\Phi_i(x_i)$ of pure tensors $\Phi_i$ each factorizable and standard outside $S$, which is pointwise real and nonnegative, and whose integral against the product of three copies of adelic additive Haar measure is positive, and every $\sigma\in\mathbb{R}$: the integral, over the quotient of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}})$ by the unipotent subgroup generated by the $u(x,y,z)$ with respect to the Haar quotient measure, of $\|W(q)\|^2\,\|\Phi(\text{third row of }q)\|\,\|\det q\|^{\sigma}$, where $W$ is the Whittaker transform of $x\mapsto\sum_i c_i f(x t_i)$ and $\|\cdot\|$ on the determinant is the idele norm, equals $\Lambda(\sigma)$ times [`WhittakerBlock.block`](def/LanglandsTunnell_CubicInduction_WhittakerBlock.html#L40) of the same data, that is the same integral restricted to the zeroth shell: the set of classes $q$ such that at every $p\notin S$ the local component of $q$ factors as an upper unipotent matrix times an element of the above local subgroup.
--
--   This is the global form of the unramified shell computation in the Rankin–Selberg integral for $\mathrm{GL}_3$: it compares the full Whittaker integral against a weight $\|\det\|^\sigma$ with the part of it supported on the classes which are unipotent times integral outside $S$, the proportionality factor $\Lambda(\sigma)\ge 1$ being determined by the shell tables $u_p$ alone. It is used in the $L^2$ theory of the slab, in particular in the construction of smoothing kernels and in the expansion of Whittaker functions under smoothing operators.
